---
<DocsLayout title="Deception as a 0-Day Sensor" description="Use the honeypot pools to catch, record and understand exploits for vulnerabilities nobody has disclosed yet" section="AI-Era Defense" sidebar={sidebar} toc={toc}>
  <p>
    Allowlisting and hardening <em>reduce</em> exposure. Deception tells you <em>what is being tried</em>.
    For AI-era attacks this matters more than ever: the first sign of a new, AI-found plugin vulnerability is
    usually an exploit attempt against someone's live shop, weeks before an advisory. PressHaven makes that
    someone's shop a recorded decoy.
  </p>

  <h2 id="sensor">A Decoy That Catches the Unknown</h2>
  <p>
    Diversion in PressHaven is triggered by <em>behaviour and context</em>, not only by known exploit
    signatures. The <a href="/docs/architecture#pipeline">routing pipeline</a> diverts on accumulated risk, IP
    reputation, admin probing, rapid automation, suspicious uploads and repeated low-grade signals (stages 3 and
    6–10), as well as CVE matches. An agent that reconnoitres before it strikes, as nearly all of them do, is
    usually diverted <em>before</em> it sends the novel exploit. The exploit then runs against an isolated,
    cloned WordPress + WooCommerce pool where:
  </p>
  <CardGrid columns={2}>
    <DocCard title="Everything is recorded" icon="eye" tag="Capture" description="Full request sequence, bodies, headers and timing reach the SIEM. The exploit chain for an undisclosed bug is in Kibana, not lost behind a 403." meta="SIEM timeline" />
    <DocCard title="Success is harmless" icon="box" tag="Isolation" description="The pool has its own database user and data copy. A working RCE or SQL injection lands on a disposable clone, and the engine retires and rebuilds it." meta="per-adversary pool" />
    <DocCard title="The attacker keeps going" icon="activity" tag="Engagement" description="The decoy looks and behaves like the real shop, so post-exploitation steps (persistence, credential theft, pivoting) are observed too." meta="full kill chain" />
    <DocCard title="Stolen secrets betray reuse" icon="key" tag="Honeytokens" description="Fake API keys, DB credentials, admin passwords, S3 URLs and JWT secrets are planted in decoys. Seeing one inbound later confirms compromise with near-zero false positives." meta="5 token types" />
  </CardGrid>

  <h2 id="signals">Signals That Need No CVE</h2>
  <table>
    <tr><th>Signal</th><th>Why it catches AI-found exploits</th><th>Status</th></tr>
    <tr><td>Generic pattern families (traversal, SQLi syntax, command injection, upload abuse)</td><td>A new bug in a new plugin is usually still exploited with an old bug <em>class</em>; the payload shape gives it away</td><td>Shipped, <a href="/docs/detection/patterns">Pattern Database</a></td></tr>
    <tr><td>Direct plugin-file access (stage 5)</td><td>Exploits often call a plugin's PHP file or AJAX action directly, which customers never do</td><td>Shipped</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Rapid automation and request-rate streaks</td><td>Agents iterate quickly on failing payloads</td><td>Shipped, <code>session_rules.lua</code></td></tr>
    <tr><td>Cumulative suspicion (stage 8)</td><td>Many small reconnaissance signals add up even when each is harmless</td><td>Shipped</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Suricata network evidence</td><td>Protocol-level anomalies independent of the WordPress code path</td><td>Shipped, <a href="/docs/detection/suricata">Suricata</a></td></tr>
    <tr><td>Unlisted route</td><td>Anything outside the shop's legitimate surface</td><td>Proposed, <a href="/docs/ai-defense/allowlisting#manifest">route manifest</a></td></tr>
    <tr><td>Honey-endpoint hit</td><td>Only an enumerating client ever requests it</td><td>Proposed, <a href="#honey">below</a></td></tr>
  </table>

  <h2 id="agents">Why Agents Fall for Decoys</h2>
  <p>LLM-driven attack agents have predictable habits that a deception layer can exploit defensively:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>They are thorough.</strong> An agent told to "find a way in" enumerates plugins, versions, users,
    backup files and config paths systematically. A human customer requests none of these.</li>
    <li><strong>They trust what they read.</strong> A version string in a <code>readme.txt</code>, a path in
    <code>robots.txt</code> or a comment in HTML is taken as fact and acted on.</li>
    <li><strong>They report success on plausible output.</strong> An agent that gets a convincing response
    (a dumped "user table", a "config file") usually moves to the next step without checking it's real. The
    next step is then observed too.</li>
    <li><strong>They are operated by people who can't verify.</strong> A low-skill operator relying on the agent
    can't tell a decoy from production. The decoy only needs to convince the model.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="honey">Honey-Endpoints (Proposed)</h2>
  <p>
    A <strong>honey-endpoint</strong> is a URL that exists only to be discovered. No page links to it and no
    customer flow uses it, so any request to it is enumeration by definition and has zero false positives from
    real customers. The proposal adds a short list served by the edge and wired to an immediate diversion with
    reason <code>honey_endpoint</code>:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>Plausible <code>robots.txt</code> <code>Disallow:</code> entries (<code>/wp-content/backup-db/</code>,
    <code>/old-admin/</code>). Crawlers that respect robots.txt never fetch them. Agents reading it as a map
    do.</li>
    <li>Classic sensitive leftovers: <code>/.env</code>, <code>/wp-config.php.bak</code>, <code>/.git/HEAD</code>.
    These are already scored by the pattern rules. Promoting them to honey-endpoints makes the first hit
    divert.</li>
    <li>A REST namespace that appears in the decoy's <code>/wp-json/</code> index but not in production's.</li>
  </ul>
  <pre><code>-- honey_endpoint_rules.lua (proposed) — pure, unit-testable
local HONEY = &#123;
  "^/wp%-content/backup%-db/",
  "^/old%-admin/",
  "^/%.git/",
  "^/wp%-config%.php%.bak$",
&#125;

function _M.is_honey_endpoint(uri)
  for _, p in ipairs(HONEY) do
    if string.find(uri, p) then return true end
  end
  return false
end</code></pre>

  <h2 id="fingerprints">Decoy Fingerprints (Proposed)</h2>
  <p>
    AI-assisted exploitation usually starts with fingerprinting: which plugins and versions are installed. Pools
    can advertise a <em>believable but deliberately outdated</em> plugin version (in <code>readme.txt</code>,
    asset <code>?ver=</code> strings and generator tags) for plugins the real shop doesn't run, while production
    advertises nothing. An agent that picks the "vulnerable" version to exploit has revealed intent and is
    already inside a decoy. Exploit attempts against these bait plugins are high-value captures: they show how
    current AI tooling weaponises a known bug class, which informs the generic rules that protect against the
    next, unknown one.
  </p>
  <div class="admonition note">
    <div class="admonition-title">Stays defensive</div>
    <p>Decoy fingerprints are content served by the defender's own decoy to clients that came to it. They don't
    plant anything on, call back to or interfere with the adversary's systems, in line with the
    <a href="/docs/reference/roadmap">roadmap's</a> no-offensive-features rule.</p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="llm">LLM-Aware Signals</h2>
  <p>Two shipped modules already look at AI-driven traffic specifically:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong><a href="/docs/detection/advanced#behaviour"><code>sophistication_analyzer.lua</code></a></strong>
    classifies each session as <code>scripted</code>, <code>ai_assisted</code>, <code>manual</code> or
    <code>unknown</code>, with every contributing signal (timing variance, SDK/agent user-agent markers, header
    completeness, precision of targeting) returned for audit. <code>ai_assisted</code> sessions are worth
    prioritising in triage: their payloads are the ones most likely to target something new.</li>
    <li><strong><code>prompt_injection_filter.lua</code></strong> scores request content that tries to manipulate an
    LLM consumer of the logs or the shop (for example an AI support bot). It contributes
    <code>risk / 2</code> to the request score. It's a detection probe. The adaptive, LLM-driven response design
    is <em>not</em> implemented.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Proposed refinements: count honey-endpoint and bait-plugin hits as strong <code>ai_assisted</code> evidence.
  Also track "systematic enumeration breadth" (distinct sensitive paths per session), which separates an agent's
  thoroughness from a commodity scanner's fixed wordlist.</p>

  <h2 id="honest">Honest Assessment</h2>
  <p><strong>Strengths:</strong> Turns attacks on undisclosed bugs into captured, replayable evidence. Isolated
  pools make a successful exploit harmless. Honeytokens give near-certain compromise confirmation. Behaviour-based
  diversion usually triggers during reconnaissance, before the novel payload.</p>
  <p><strong>Limitations:</strong> A careful attacker who skips reconnaissance and sends a single well-formed
  exploit from a clean IP to an allowed route reaches production. Deception only helps once something has
  diverted the session. Honey-endpoints and decoy fingerprints are proposals. The sophistication classifier is
  rule-based and can be fooled by an agent that mimics browser headers and human timing. Pool capacity is finite
  under mass exploitation (see <a href="/docs/engine#pools">pool limits</a>).</p>
</DocsLayout>
